That matches what I have. Good.

For setattro, I'm not 100% sure what upstream does. But it's a reasonable guard. Let me check whether there could be any test that expects `del field` to work (e.g., deleting a Python attribute set on the object). In `JObj_setattro`, the guard only triggers when `oldValue` is a JField (i.e., a Java field). If a user sets a Python attribute on the object (non-JField), it goes to `PyObject_GenericSetAttr` with NULL which handles it properly. So the guard only affects Java fields, which can't be deleted anyway. Good.

